  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2ee87e681215b6147a44021cf30db039, type: 3}
  m_Name: 2.M&S
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Marks and Spencer
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.513548, y: 35.898964}
  firstPartInfo: "This square is lined with shops on both ends, but none of them
    are a pharmacy.\r\nOne of these shops must have replaced it, so I look up a brief
    description of each of their histories until I find a connection with Marks and
    Spencer to the Tayar name\r."
  secondPartInfo: "Looking further into the history of this business, I discover
    that a Tayar family member, Joseph Tayar, owned a leather goods business in St
    George\u2019s Square. It was later handed down to his son, Jacob Tayar, who converted
    it into a tailor\u2019s shop  called Tayar's Sportex and Tweed . Ondina and her
    sister Margot Tayar then took over the shop after Jacob Tayar died until the
    shop was bought by Marks and Spencer, which at the time was still under the name
    St. Michael\u2019s.  Not only that, but Joseph Tayar was also the first Rabbi
    for the Maltese Jewish community after the community was re-established in the
    nineteenth century, following the expulsion of Jews from Malta in 1492, Although
    these are relatives of Ondina, there is no mention of her. From what I have found
    about her so far, I only know that she was a pharmacist. How did she become one?"
  imgMarker: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c98feaebd40d8df449e6d3855ac84352, type: 3}
  imgMain: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f26fa8880b3aeef4b8516c82dc12f2a5, type: 3}
  nameOfStory: Jewish
  hintOfPOI: Where did people in the 1900s used to get educated to get qualified
    to become pharmacists?
  solutionOfPOI: "I know that there is an old university in the next street. It\u2019s
    near the end of St Paul Street."
